        <p class="lead"><strong>A bank statement analyzer reads a statement you already have, sorts every transaction into spending categories, and flags the charges that repeat, and the Best Answer Hub Bank Statement Analyzer does all of it in your browser, so your statement is never uploaded and your bank account is never linked.</strong> This guide covers why forgotten subscriptions cost more than people expect, how the categorizing works and why it is worth checking, how price rises hide in plain sight, and why reading one statement locally is safer than granting an app standing access to your account.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub Bank Statement Analyzer?</h2>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/bank-statement-analyzer/">Bank Statement Analyzer</a> is a single-page tool that takes a bank or credit card statement, as a PDF or CSV, and turns it into a picture of your spending: it sorts transactions into twelve categories, detects recurring charges and subscriptions with a confidence level, lists your top merchants, estimates the monthly and annual cost of each subscription, and flags price increases and duplicate services. You can correct any category from a dropdown, export a categorized CSV, and download a renewal calendar as an .ics file. Everything runs on your device, so the statement is never uploaded, never stored, and no bank login is ever connected. It needs no account, adds no watermark, and shows no ads. The tool sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/finance-toolkit/">Finance Toolkit</a> and the wider <a href="/tools/">Tools hub</a>, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising.</p>

        <div class="cav">The money hook</div>
        <h2 id="cost">Why do forgotten subscriptions cost so much?</h2>
        <p>Because they are small, regular, and easy to lose track of, so the total is far bigger than people guess. When C+R Research asked 1,000 US consumers to estimate their monthly subscription spend, the average guess was about 86 dollars, but when the same people added up their actual subscriptions, the real figure was about 219 dollars, a gap of 133 dollars a month, or roughly 1,600 dollars a year (<a href="https://www.crresearch.com/blog/subscription-service-statistics-and-costs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">C+R Research, 2022</a>). In the same survey, 42 percent admitted they were still paying for a subscription they had stopped using, and 74 percent said it is easy to forget a recurring monthly charge. A more recent survey put real money on the waste: US adults spend about 17 dollars a month on subscriptions they do not use, and Gen Z the most (<a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/news/subscriptions-control-cnet-survey-shows-110000941.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CNET/YouGov, 2025</a>). Seeing the real total on your own statement is the fix, and that is what the Best Answer Hub Bank Statement Analyzer is built to show.</p>
        <blockquote>A subscription you forgot is still a subscription you are paying for. <span class="accent">The fix is not more willpower, it is seeing the charge.</span></blockquote>
