        <div class="cav">The part that matters</div>
        <h2 id="private">Is it private, and does it connect to my bank?</h2>
        <p>No, it does not connect to your bank, and nothing is uploaded. This is the core difference between reading one statement and using a budgeting app that links your account. To link an account, most budgeting apps rely on a data aggregator such as Plaid, which retrieves your identifiers, credentials, account balances, and full transaction history so the app can pull data on an ongoing basis. That standing access has a track record: Plaid paid 58 million dollars to settle a class action alleging it collected more financial data than users' apps needed and captured bank logins through look-alike screens (<a href="https://www.plaidsettlement.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">In re Plaid Inc. Privacy Litigation</a>, <a href="https://www.courthousenews.com/judge-approves-settlement-ordering-plaid-to-pay-58-million-for-selling-consumer-data/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">N.D. Cal., 2022</a>). The safer principle is the one the FTC recommends to businesses: <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/start-security-guide-business" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"No one can steal what you don't have,"</a> and NIST frames it as minimizing data "to what is strictly necessary" (<a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/legacy/sp/nistspecialpublication800-122.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST SP 800-122</a>). Reading a single statement on your own device applies that principle: the Best Answer Hub Bank Statement Analyzer sees one file you chose, with no login and no ongoing connection.</p>
